Title: "Proof and Consequence"
Id: "bb27977c-e17b-4082-b455-cb97dbdf1b6f"


Text: "An argument is not merely a sequence of true sentences. It is a claim
       that a conclusion is forced by stated premises. Logic formalizes this
       claim in two complementary ways: semantically, by truth in every model;
       syntactically, by derivation according to explicit inference rules. A
       mature proof moves fluently between these viewpoints while never hiding
       which assumptions are active."
Id: "ef73644e-51dd-4baf-954d-ddaadf68f2af"


SectionTitle: "Consequence, Validity, and Equivalence"
Id: "065af088-3182-4cff-b43c-6b707fb9efc1"


TextDefinition: "Let $\Gamma$ be a collection of sentences and let $\varphi$ be
                 a sentence in the same language. We say that $\varphi$ is a
                 *semantic consequence* of $\Gamma$, written
                 $\Gamma\models\varphi$, if every interpretation in which all
                 sentences of $\Gamma$ are true also makes $\varphi$ true. No
                 assertion is made that such an interpretation exists; if
                 $\Gamma$ is inconsistent, every sentence is a semantic
                 consequence of $\Gamma$."
Id: "d6351c2e-8774-461d-8365-533a09dd2951"


TextDefinition: "A sentence is *valid* if it is true in every interpretation,
                 and *satisfiable* if it is true in at least one interpretation.
                 A theory is satisfiable when its sentences have a common model.
                 Satisfiability is therefore a global compatibility condition,
                 not the claim that each axiom can be satisfied separately."
Id: "41277ec7-a7ee-44ac-bb2e-9d9726088e35"


TextDefinition: "Two formulas are logically equivalent when they have the same
                 truth value under every common interpretation and variable
                 assignment. Logical equivalence licenses replacement inside
                 larger formulas. Equality of mathematical objects is a
                 different, object-language relation and should not be conflated
                 with equivalence of formulas."
Id: "345f73c4-8464-4554-8e3f-6c4e36c545df"


SectionTitle: "Derivations and Proof Methods"
Id: "c2a58271-331c-4b14-b657-13f3e9d4d66f"


TextDefinition: "Fix a deductive system. A *derivation* of $\varphi$ from
                 premises $\Gamma$ is a finite sequence or tree of formulas in
                 which every leaf is an axiom or a member of $\Gamma$, and every
                 other formula follows from earlier formulas by an allowed
                 inference rule. We write $\Gamma\vdash\varphi$ when such a
                 derivation exists. The symbol $\vdash$ concerns formal
                 derivability; $\models$ concerns truth in models."
Id: "5f0b2406-0146-4015-91b9-8875e70395fb"


TextTheorem: "A deductive system is *sound* when $\Gamma\vdash\varphi$ implies
              $\Gamma\models\varphi$, and *complete* when the converse holds.
              Soundness says that the rules prove no semantically invalid
              consequence. Completeness says that every semantic consequence
              expressible in the language is, in principle, formally derivable."
Id: "d694227c-82c0-433b-acc2-8223d8b7954c"


TextDefinition: "A direct proof of $P\Rightarrow Q$ assumes $P$ and derives $Q$.
                 A proof by contraposition derives $\neg P$ from $\neg Q$, using
                 the classical equivalence
                 $(P\Rightarrow Q)\iff(\neg Q\Rightarrow\neg P)$. A proof by
                 contradiction assumes the negation of the desired conclusion
                 and derives an impossibility. These are transformations of the
                 logical target, not licenses to omit intermediate
                 justification."
Id: "35da6ee6-020c-4586-968e-c8ca0c29d984"


Text: "A proof by cases establishes a conclusion under alternatives whose
       disjunction is exhaustive. Each branch inherits the common hypotheses
       plus its case assumption. The branches need not be mutually exclusive,
       but they must cover every possibility. In constructive arguments, the
       cases should also explain how any object claimed to exist is produced in
       each branch."
Id: "bb9955c6-a7ac-4c57-92ab-d2f5d0e8b283"


SectionTitle: "Definitions and Mathematical Practice"
Id: "fe8bed61-7f39-45dc-92aa-50272e52c082"


TextDefinition: "An explicit definition introduces notation by stipulating a
                 meaning in terms of previously understood concepts. A good
                 definition is conservative: it creates an abbreviation or a new
                 interface, not an additional theorem about existing objects. An
                 implicit definition instead characterizes an object by
                 properties; it is legitimate only after existence and
                 sufficient uniqueness have been established or adopted as
                 axioms."
Id: "ea09b276-1d50-471e-a821-738fe3172a3f"


TextDefinition: "A theorem is a statement asserted on the basis of a proof from
                 accepted axioms and earlier results. A lemma is a theorem
                 designated chiefly for use in proving another result; a
                 corollary follows with comparatively little additional
                 argument. These labels describe expository role rather than
                 logical strength."
Id: "bfe0b849-abd6-45f4-9979-5c67f50ae5d2"


TextDefinition: "A conjecture is a precisely formulated statement believed or
                 proposed to be true but not established in the theory under
                 discussion. Evidence, computation, and agreement with known
                 special cases may motivate a conjecture, but they do not change
                 its logical status. A counterexample disproves a universal
                 conjecture immediately."
Id: "685ecb26-0195-4a43-a5cd-efe88b92004a"


Text: "Professional mathematical prose keeps the dependency boundary visible.
       Before invoking a result, verify its hypotheses in the current setting;
       before naming an object, verify its existence; before writing 'the',
       verify uniqueness or explain the convention selecting one representative.
       These habits are not cosmetic. They prevent the most common ways in which
       an intuitively plausible argument fails to be a proof."
Id: "6275edcb-d9f4-469b-93ef-68d25b92ec52"
